1
00:00:05,137 --> 00:00:07,530
- All, not just in the education sector,

2
00:00:07,530 --> 00:00:09,570
but just overall, all around.

3
00:00:09,570 --> 00:00:10,800
And this is one way we can do that

4
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:13,323
by preserving what we're all worried about, right?

5
00:00:14,460 --> 00:00:17,070
We're gonna lose our credibility for critical thinking,

6
00:00:17,070 --> 00:00:20,910
and, you know, our ability to be unique in society

7
00:00:20,910 --> 00:00:22,380
and all that stuff, even though, you know,

8
00:00:22,380 --> 00:00:23,957
don't know that we have that much control of it.

9
00:00:23,957 --> 00:00:25,980
(panelist laughs)

10
00:00:25,980 --> 00:00:28,860
It's really moving towards that progress

11
00:00:28,860 --> 00:00:33,810
with humans at the center of it all, truly, truly,

12
00:00:33,810 --> 00:00:36,633
to really preserve any of these human elements.

13
00:00:38,010 --> 00:00:39,150
Yeah.

14
00:00:39,150 --> 00:00:41,043
- I actually wanna add to this.

15
00:00:43,140 --> 00:00:45,330
I think a lot of us, and a lot of us here,

16
00:00:45,330 --> 00:00:49,020
you know, we teach first-generation college students

17
00:00:49,020 --> 00:00:54,020
who went to schools where they were told what to write,

18
00:00:54,090 --> 00:00:57,543
when to write, how many words, spacing.

19
00:00:58,410 --> 00:01:01,170
I mean, in a way, they were told what to think.

20
00:01:01,170 --> 00:01:05,217
So, when you actually ask them, "What do you think?"

21
00:01:06,487 --> 00:01:08,190
"I don't know, what should I think?"

22
00:01:08,190 --> 00:01:10,260
- Mm-hm. - Okay?

23
00:01:10,260 --> 00:01:11,407
So, that whole idea of like,

24
00:01:11,407 --> 00:01:15,063
you know, how do we teach students to read critically,

25
00:01:15,930 --> 00:01:17,430
you know, that's a struggle.

26
00:01:17,430 --> 00:01:20,910
We have to come up with creative tools.

27
00:01:20,910 --> 00:01:24,603
And this is where I'm gonna do a plugin for like Playlab.

28
00:01:25,620 --> 00:01:28,260
If you don't know what Playlab is,

29
00:01:28,260 --> 00:01:30,990
it's a chatbot that you build.

30
00:01:30,990 --> 00:01:34,503
Your corazon, your heart builds it.

31
00:01:35,520 --> 00:01:39,000
You build that, as we call it, vibe coding,

32
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:42,870
or that narrative of how you want it to perform,

33
00:01:42,870 --> 00:01:44,910
how you want it to help.

34
00:01:44,910 --> 00:01:49,910
And so for me, for example, I know I think of myself

35
00:01:49,980 --> 00:01:51,060
when it's like, you know,

36
00:01:51,060 --> 00:01:53,580
that story I gave you about Salomon Huerta,

37
00:01:53,580 --> 00:01:56,040
I was looking at his art, okay?

38
00:01:56,040 --> 00:01:58,290
He actually got a MacArthur Genius Award

39
00:01:58,290 --> 00:01:59,883
and I didn't get it.

40
00:02:00,900 --> 00:02:03,630
It's like, when he asked me, "What do you think?"

41
00:02:03,630 --> 00:02:06,603
I was like, "I don't know," okay?

42
00:02:08,280 --> 00:02:12,240
But when he explained to me, the process, I was able to,

43
00:02:12,240 --> 00:02:14,310
you know, come up with an answer.

44
00:02:14,310 --> 00:02:17,430
So with PlayLab, you know, I built the mechanism

45
00:02:17,430 --> 00:02:22,430
to enable students to ask it, to talk to the student,

46
00:02:22,590 --> 00:02:27,180
according to their passions, according to their hobbies,

47
00:02:27,180 --> 00:02:28,800
according to what they're into.

48
00:02:28,800 --> 00:02:31,980
For example, if a student is a basketball player,

49
00:02:31,980 --> 00:02:34,800
they can talk to our chatbot that's connected

50
00:02:34,800 --> 00:02:39,360
to our textbook and say, "Look, I play basketball.

51
00:02:39,360 --> 00:02:41,640
Professor's asking me about intersectionality.

52
00:02:41,640 --> 00:02:43,167
I don't really get it."

53
00:02:44,070 --> 00:02:45,513
And guess what it'll do.

54
00:02:46,800 --> 00:02:48,030
It'll talk to the student.

55
00:02:48,030 --> 00:02:49,920
It'll actually be like, "Oh, don't worry.

56
00:02:49,920 --> 00:02:51,420
I know it's hard."

57
00:02:51,420 --> 00:02:54,240
Well, I hear the voice in my head.

58
00:02:54,240 --> 00:02:57,180
You know, I wish it could have the little avatar

59
00:02:57,180 --> 00:02:58,530
'cause it'd be like, "Ah, don't worry.

60
00:02:58,530 --> 00:03:00,147
That's a fancy word, isn't it?"

61
00:03:01,020 --> 00:03:01,853
You know?

62
00:03:03,210 --> 00:03:07,449
And so I added those like vernacular words,

63
00:03:07,449 --> 00:03:12,449
even some Gen Z you know, lingo to be like, you know,

64
00:03:12,630 --> 00:03:14,880
you know what you're saying is, you know, is quite cap.

65
00:03:14,880 --> 00:03:17,280
But, you know, I don't also wanna be ingenuous.

66
00:03:17,280 --> 00:03:18,870
I do wanna say, I don't wanna be ingenuous.

67
00:03:18,870 --> 00:03:20,493
I've been struggling with that.

68
00:03:23,010 --> 00:03:25,200
But my point is, is that this is where,

69
00:03:25,200 --> 00:03:28,320
as practitioners, we can use a tool

70
00:03:28,320 --> 00:03:30,690
to help the students be able to get to that point

71
00:03:30,690 --> 00:03:33,870
where they can just say, is it good?

72
00:03:33,870 --> 00:03:38,870
And if it's not, why do you feel this way, you know?

73
00:03:40,170 --> 00:03:43,980
And so that is still a challenge for a lot of our students.

74
00:03:43,980 --> 00:03:46,440
That's just critical thinking is still something

75
00:03:46,440 --> 00:03:48,030
that they struggle with.

76
00:03:48,030 --> 00:03:49,560
I've said this story before, like,

77
00:03:49,560 --> 00:03:52,230
my sister was a straight A student in high school,

78
00:03:52,230 --> 00:03:55,529
but when she went into college, she started failing

79
00:03:55,529 --> 00:03:58,050
'cause my sister, she would memorize.

80
00:03:58,050 --> 00:04:00,630
She was one of those that would spend hours memorizing

81
00:04:00,630 --> 00:04:02,610
and would get straight A's.

82
00:04:02,610 --> 00:04:03,720
When she went to college,

83
00:04:03,720 --> 00:04:06,837
she couldn't answer the question, "What do you think?"

84
00:04:08,550 --> 00:04:11,160
And like, "What do you mean, what do I think?"

85
00:04:11,160 --> 00:04:14,580
So, just that idea of just the critical thinking,

86
00:04:14,580 --> 00:04:16,860
like you said, is still something, you know,

87
00:04:16,860 --> 00:04:17,940
we struggle with.

88
00:04:17,940 --> 00:04:19,110
- Yeah, I think that's interesting

89
00:04:19,110 --> 00:04:22,923
because one thing that I'm doing with my students now is,

90
00:04:23,820 --> 00:04:25,500
because there is AI

91
00:04:25,500 --> 00:04:28,440
and it's about them being able to assess the output

92
00:04:28,440 --> 00:04:31,080
that AI is giving them and all that,

93
00:04:31,080 --> 00:04:33,210
but what kind of a thinker are you?

94
00:04:33,210 --> 00:04:35,220
Are you waiting to be told what to think?

95
00:04:35,220 --> 00:04:37,050
Are you a blank page that's waiting

96
00:04:37,050 --> 00:04:38,700
to have an answer input?

97
00:04:38,700 --> 00:04:40,110
Do you have an answer?

98
00:04:40,110 --> 00:04:42,810
I'm trying to see what other people say so you can decide

99
00:04:42,810 --> 00:04:44,490
what you think about your answer.

100
00:04:44,490 --> 00:04:45,780
You know, kind of that back and forth.

101
00:04:45,780 --> 00:04:47,490
You're waiting for that.

102
00:04:47,490 --> 00:04:52,410
And so I am trying to cultivate like, okay, let's be aware

103
00:04:52,410 --> 00:04:54,450
of our thinking style, right?

104
00:04:54,450 --> 00:04:57,300
'Cause that's gonna matter when you're looking at AI,

105
00:04:57,300 --> 00:05:00,180
like when I enter stuff in AI, I tend

106
00:05:00,180 --> 00:05:02,700
to have an answer already in my head, right?

107
00:05:02,700 --> 00:05:05,520
And if it's contrary to my answer, like, okay, but why?

108
00:05:05,520 --> 00:05:08,460
If it makes a really good point, you know, I'm willing

109
00:05:08,460 --> 00:05:10,650
to change my response.

110
00:05:10,650 --> 00:05:12,870
You know, and I think that is important

111
00:05:12,870 --> 00:05:15,780
in critical thinking is like when you find a flaw

112
00:05:15,780 --> 00:05:17,730
in your own perfect answer,

113
00:05:17,730 --> 00:05:20,430
being willing to admit that and, you know, that's okay

114
00:05:20,430 --> 00:05:23,070
to like, oh yeah, okay, that makes more sense.

115
00:05:23,070 --> 00:05:25,860
And so for me, I think it's really curiosity too.

116
00:05:25,860 --> 00:05:27,330
You have to have curiosity.

117
00:05:27,330 --> 00:05:29,700
You have to crave to wanna know, right?

118
00:05:29,700 --> 00:05:32,430
And so, one way that I would say

119
00:05:32,430 --> 00:05:37,430
as an educator is currently, I'm trying to help my students

120
00:05:38,700 --> 00:05:41,640
and I don't know, I feel, again, I feel a little like,

121
00:05:41,640 --> 00:05:46,640
you know, arrogant to be like, I'm helping them, you know,

122
00:05:46,680 --> 00:05:49,380
understand their thinking process, right?

123
00:05:49,380 --> 00:05:50,910
Because maybe they do on some level,

124
00:05:50,910 --> 00:05:52,350
and I don't wanna dismiss that,

125
00:05:52,350 --> 00:05:54,390
but being aware of it too, just like, okay,

126
00:05:54,390 --> 00:05:56,760
well what kind of a thinker are you, right,

127
00:05:56,760 --> 00:05:59,010
before you start jumping into AI,

128
00:05:59,010 --> 00:06:00,600
'cause I fall into that trap with Ai.

129
00:06:00,600 --> 00:06:02,460
Sometimes, I love AI

130
00:06:02,460 --> 00:06:04,830
'cause they give me answers really quick, right?

131
00:06:04,830 --> 00:06:06,093
Like right away.

132
00:06:07,290 --> 00:06:11,100
And sometimes I don't even trust myself doing that

133
00:06:11,100 --> 00:06:14,310
because I'm like, but now I don't know what I was thinking.

134
00:06:14,310 --> 00:06:15,143
- Yeah. - Right.

135
00:06:15,143 --> 00:06:18,330
Like now I don't know if that was my thought process.

136
00:06:18,330 --> 00:06:20,340
And I like to know my thought process.

137
00:06:20,340 --> 00:06:23,460
I like to know if it's coming from what I'm thinking of.

138
00:06:23,460 --> 00:06:24,293
You know?

139
00:06:25,500 --> 00:06:27,033
- Did you find the word?

140
00:06:30,180 --> 00:06:31,710
What was it?

141
00:06:31,710 --> 00:06:33,369
- [Speaker] I'm sorry, and you were looking for?

142
00:06:33,369 --> 00:06:36,330
- The word, the psychological term?

143
00:06:36,330 --> 00:06:40,109
- [Speaker] Oh, it might have been in a different article.

144
00:06:40,109 --> 00:06:42,510
Were you thinking about the one where the student

145
00:06:42,510 --> 00:06:44,470
or the child who actually committed suicide?

146
00:06:44,470 --> 00:06:45,303
- No. - No, no.

147
00:06:45,303 --> 00:06:46,136
- [Speaker] That's a different one.

148
00:06:46,136 --> 00:06:47,553
- No, no, no. Oh, darn.

149
00:06:48,461 --> 00:06:49,980
- Could I say something?

150
00:06:49,980 --> 00:06:52,020
- Yeah, what were you saying, Wendy, you know.

151
00:06:52,020 --> 00:06:52,890
- Yeah. - Yeah, go for it.

152
00:06:52,890 --> 00:06:55,530
- I mean, there's an insidious dimension here, though.

153
00:06:55,530 --> 00:06:58,080
I wanna be slightly contrarian.

154
00:06:58,080 --> 00:07:01,890
Just like, I worry about,

155
00:07:01,890 --> 00:07:05,880
so when expert practitioners use these systems,

156
00:07:05,880 --> 00:07:09,450
like us, we have years

157
00:07:09,450 --> 00:07:12,660
of training without these technologies that we can use

158
00:07:12,660 --> 00:07:13,830
as our basis.

159
00:07:13,830 --> 00:07:17,130
And I worry, you know, like for your students, for instance,

160
00:07:17,130 --> 00:07:19,380
like there is something I believe

161
00:07:19,380 --> 00:07:23,190
about that cognitive friction of encountering something

162
00:07:23,190 --> 00:07:27,439
that is difficult and pushing into it and past it

163
00:07:27,439 --> 00:07:29,070
that's really important

164
00:07:29,070 --> 00:07:32,310
for building your own self-confidence.

165
00:07:32,310 --> 00:07:34,020
And so I worry sometimes that, like,

166
00:07:34,020 --> 00:07:36,090
when do the training wheels come off?

167
00:07:36,090 --> 00:07:37,080
You know, that's, I think,

168
00:07:37,080 --> 00:07:38,880
the thing we're still not sure about.

169
00:07:38,880 --> 00:07:41,670
And I worry in the same way that, you know,

170
00:07:41,670 --> 00:07:45,300
we didn't know really that social media would have all

171
00:07:45,300 --> 00:07:46,680
of these consequences.

172
00:07:46,680 --> 00:07:51,470
So, I feel like we're experimenting with our kids sometimes.

173
00:07:51,470 --> 00:07:55,110
I mean, we sort of have no choice.

174
00:07:55,110 --> 00:07:58,770
But as humanists, we are very good at thinking

175
00:07:58,770 --> 00:08:01,080
in four different ways, simultaneously.

176
00:08:01,080 --> 00:08:03,600
And I think we have to keep that, you know,

177
00:08:03,600 --> 00:08:05,460
Jiminy Cricket, right,

178
00:08:05,460 --> 00:08:06,693
- Oh, yeah. - In mind.

179
00:08:07,560 --> 00:08:10,380
- With that in mind, I wanna ask the last question,

180
00:08:10,380 --> 00:08:15,380
which is, in what ways can humanities provide a guiding lens

181
00:08:15,630 --> 00:08:20,463
to how AI is integrated into education and society?

182
00:08:23,280 --> 00:08:25,950
- I think you had a brilliant idea on the critical STS.

183
00:08:25,950 --> 00:08:27,060
- Okay. - I'm unaware of it.

184
00:08:27,060 --> 00:08:29,080
So I would love for you to take the lead on this.

185
00:08:29,080 --> 00:08:32,010
- Okay, I made this one comment

186
00:08:32,010 --> 00:08:34,770
about how I'd make a plug for a whole discipline,

187
00:08:34,770 --> 00:08:39,750
which is science technology studies, STS,

188
00:08:39,750 --> 00:08:41,310
and critical STS,

189
00:08:41,310 --> 00:08:44,190
which has really critiqued the scientific development

190
00:08:44,190 --> 00:08:46,440
in the 19th and 20th century.

191
00:08:46,440 --> 00:08:50,880
And it really helped to help us see

192
00:08:50,880 --> 00:08:52,830
and historicize the ways

193
00:08:52,830 --> 00:08:55,560
in which this was a sociological phenomenon.

194
00:08:55,560 --> 00:08:57,930
So I think that there are frameworks

195
00:08:57,930 --> 00:08:58,763
that come from there.

196
00:08:58,763 --> 00:09:00,930
I'll just mention a couple books and I'll say something.

197
00:09:00,930 --> 00:09:03,997
So, you know, one that people probably know is,

198
00:09:03,997 --> 00:09:07,110
"Design Justice" by Costanza-Chock.

199
00:09:07,110 --> 00:09:09,510
But there's also this great book

200
00:09:09,510 --> 00:09:13,047
by Arturo Escobar, "Designs for the Pluriverse."

201
00:09:14,220 --> 00:09:18,030
And then the other thing, I have some more,

202
00:09:18,030 --> 00:09:20,820
like "Race After Technology," Ruha Benjamin's classic book

203
00:09:20,820 --> 00:09:22,920
and "Automating Inequality."

204
00:09:22,920 --> 00:09:26,850
But I also have become more aware

205
00:09:26,850 --> 00:09:28,590
of other dimensions.

206
00:09:28,590 --> 00:09:30,690
My colleague, Megan Moody,

207
00:09:30,690 --> 00:09:33,510
Santa Cruz works in disability studies.

208
00:09:33,510 --> 00:09:35,340
And with her, I've been reading a lot

209
00:09:35,340 --> 00:09:37,200
of work in critical disability studies,

210
00:09:37,200 --> 00:09:41,040
and she's made the point to me that, you know, with these,

211
00:09:41,040 --> 00:09:45,060
you know, future AI individuals, we all have a kind

212
00:09:45,060 --> 00:09:46,740
of disability with respect to it.

213
00:09:46,740 --> 00:09:49,410
And so disability studies, which emphasizes the way

214
00:09:49,410 --> 00:09:53,640
in which people as individuals navigate a world

215
00:09:53,640 --> 00:09:57,660
that isn't designed for them, is a really effective lens

216
00:09:57,660 --> 00:09:58,860
for thinking about the future.

217
00:09:58,860 --> 00:10:01,110
So there's a book that she recommended

218
00:10:01,110 --> 00:10:04,020
by Sara Hendren, called, "What Can a Body Do:

219
00:10:04,020 --> 00:10:05,700
How We Meet the Built World,"

220
00:10:05,700 --> 00:10:08,640
that has been very helpful for me in thinking about things.

221
00:10:08,640 --> 00:10:10,860
And I should just say, as humanists, one thing

222
00:10:10,860 --> 00:10:14,070
that we're really good at is thinking about corner cases,

223
00:10:14,070 --> 00:10:17,670
thinking about individual instances,

224
00:10:17,670 --> 00:10:20,400
thinking about the long tail, right?

225
00:10:20,400 --> 00:10:21,810
And that is not something

226
00:10:21,810 --> 00:10:25,140
that people in the AI industry really care about,

227
00:10:25,140 --> 00:10:27,660
but we should care about because we care about people.

228
00:10:27,660 --> 00:10:30,750
And all of these scholars really reckon

229
00:10:30,750 --> 00:10:33,060
with the way the technology,

230
00:10:33,060 --> 00:10:36,330
as a discipline, hasn't thought about its impacts

231
00:10:36,330 --> 00:10:38,550
on individual people.

232
00:10:38,550 --> 00:10:41,190
And that's something that we need to bring again

233
00:10:41,190 --> 00:10:42,750
and again to our students.

234
00:10:42,750 --> 00:10:45,690
And it's something that these books and their frameworks,

235
00:10:45,690 --> 00:10:48,750
but also narrative is so powerful for.

236
00:10:48,750 --> 00:10:51,870
There's a reason why we crave narrative, right?

237
00:10:51,870 --> 00:10:54,539
Because it makes sense to us in a way.

238
00:10:54,539 --> 00:10:55,372
- Yeah.

239
00:10:55,372 --> 00:10:58,260
Yeah, so because this is about humanities,

240
00:10:58,260 --> 00:11:00,870
just one fun last question.

241
00:11:00,870 --> 00:11:02,523
What brings you joy, Rachel?

242
00:11:03,720 --> 00:11:06,930
- That's a simple one. It's just my family and my dogs.

243
00:11:06,930 --> 00:11:07,862
- Yay. Family and dogs.

244
00:11:07,862 --> 00:11:09,450
- Spending time with them.

245
00:11:09,450 --> 00:11:11,160
- What brings you joy?

246
00:11:11,160 --> 00:11:13,200
- What brings me joy? I don't know.

247
00:11:13,200 --> 00:11:15,263
I really like making art. - Okay.

248
00:11:16,560 --> 00:11:17,393
Ah, right.

249
00:11:17,393 --> 00:11:19,366
- We'll let your family know they weren't on the top.

250
00:11:19,366 --> 00:11:20,760
(audience laughing)

251
00:11:20,760 --> 00:11:21,869
- They already know.

252
00:11:21,869 --> 00:11:23,010
(everyone laughing)

253
00:11:23,010 --> 00:11:24,090
- I have a new joy,

254
00:11:24,090 --> 00:11:26,070
and it was introduced to me from Michelle, like,

255
00:11:26,070 --> 00:11:29,580
I wanna look for daily awe moments.

256
00:11:29,580 --> 00:11:31,590
So, I'm like taking my camera

257
00:11:31,590 --> 00:11:34,410
and taking pictures of like, "Oh, look at this flower.

258
00:11:34,410 --> 00:11:35,310
Wow."

